## Distilled lessons from prior iterations (cores/bench/LESSONS.md)
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product and deriving MULH/MULHSU with sign-correction subtractions preserves RV32M results while eliminating duplicated multiplier hardware without adding pipeline latency.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Splitting EX into EX1/EX2 changes producer availability, so forwarding, valid/flush, and load-use hazard logic must be derived from the exact new stage timing or dependent instructions can corrupt architectural register writes.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -12.37%): Static BTFNT in IF was too aggressive for CoreMark—backward-branch exits and weakly taken backward branches trigger EX-time redirect/flush penalties, so fetch-side speculation must be benchmark-profiled or made selective.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Gating load-use stalls with decoder-generated rs1/rs2-use metadata can remove a mandatory interlock and corrupt architectural registers; operand-use classification must be proven against every instruction's actual reads and IF/ID validity.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.29%): A two-entry instruction prefetch queue with an independent fetch PC lets instruction-bus backpressure overlap backend/data stalls, but redirect handling must atomically invalidate queued wrong-path entries.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -46.80%): A direct-mapped BTB/BHT can remain functionally correct yet lose heavily when its combinational tag/counter lookup and predicted-target mux sit on the IF next-PC path, so predictor timing must be isolated or kept off the fetch critical path.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.84%): Writing shared signed divide/remainder selection around separate unsigned `/` and `%` operators still computes both wide cones and adds magnitude/sign-correction logic, so RTL-level operand sharing is not sufficient to reduce divider cost without an explicitly shared or iterative datapath.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (regression, -29.56%): Deepening the prefetch queue to a four-entry circular FIFO regressed despite preserving streaming semantics, showing that the indexed head mux and added pointer/count control can outweigh stall absorption when extra buffering is not the bottleneck.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -25.79%): Wrapping a 64-bit multiply result with explicitly 32-bit unsigned temporaries did not guarantee a narrower synthesized multiplier and sharply regressed CoreMark, so operand-width intent must be validated against post-synthesis timing and area rather than inferred from RTL types alone.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Even a one-entry registered normal-RAM store buffer can exhaust Tang Nano 20K placement headroom—the added bus mux, buffer state, and hazard control left no BELs for the existing `dmem` `RAM16SDP4`, so store decoupling must be checked against post-synthesis resource margin rather than functional correctness alone.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): An early direct-JAL redirect must update the prefetch queue and independent `fetch_pc_q` from the same predicted target; splitting `predict_fire` from that atomic state transition can desynchronize fetch and corrupt CoreMark.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -17.77%): Explicitly naming a shared `pc + imm` target in `always_comb` did not guarantee EX adder/routing reduction and regressed CoreMark, so datapath factoring must be validated after synthesis rather than assumed from RTL common-subexpression structure.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (regression, -5.85%): A 32-iteration DIV/REM EX sidecar can remove the combinational divider cone yet still lose on CoreMark because holding the in-order pipeline behind `divider_busy` serializes each relatively rare divide for its full latency; multi-cycle arithmetic must earn back its IPC cost in measured Fmax.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): Instantiating a new `branch_predictor` in `core.sv` without adding its RTL implementation to the build makes cosimulation fail before predictor behavior is tested, so pipeline experiments must verify module and source-list integration first.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (regression, -36.41%): Replacing 2-bit forwarding selects with 32-bit priority-selected operands regressed CoreMark because moving the value muxing into the hazard network increased EX-path fanout/routing, so forwarding refactors must be judged on post-synthesis timing rather than mux-count alone.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A direct MEM-to-EX load bypass must release the load-use interlock only when the same-cycle `dmem_ready` response is valid and aligned; gating the hazard solely on producer alignment can let dependents advance without a usable load value and break cosimulation.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A predicted queue-head redirect must use one effective target consistently for `imem_addr`, `fetch_pc_q`, and the queued entry PC; mixing the predicted target with EX's resolved `redirect_target` corrupts branch RVFI PCs and fails `pc_fwd`.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -7.59%): Replacing restricted-shift load extraction with nested explicit lane cases regressed CoreMark, showing that extra MEM-stage muxing and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings and must be validated after synthesis.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): An early-resolved conditional branch must carry its resolved target/next-PC marker through ID/EX and suppress EX-time re-resolution, or duplicate redirect and RVFI pc_next bookkeeping can fail pc_fwd even when the other formal checks pass.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (regression, -16.09%): Sharing branch predicates through a 33-bit subtract still regressed CoreMark because the subtract, signed-sign logic, and predicate selection increased EX redirect-path timing/routing, so comparator sharing must be validated after synthesis rather than assumed to reduce cones.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Predecoded control and immediates become part of a fetch-queue entry's identity, so every push/pop, simultaneous refill, redirect refill, and flush must carry them atomically with the raw instruction and PC or formal instruction/RVFI checks fail across the ISA.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -19.00%): Moving the PC+4 adder from EX into ID and carrying a 32-bit result through ID/EX regressed CoreMark, showing that stage relocation can shift timing/routing pressure into decode and bundle transport instead of shortening the critical path.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -32.94%): Replacing compact two-entry queue counting with explicit valid-bit pop/push promotion logic regressed CoreMark, showing that extra slot-valid decode and simultaneous-transition muxing can outweigh removing small arithmetic on FPGA fetch control paths.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (broken, n/a): Prediction-mismatch redirect logic must retain the actual `branch_taken` qualification for unpredicted conditional branches; redirecting every unpredicted branch, including not-taken ones, sends fetch to arbitrary or misaligned branch targets and fails RVFI trap/PC checks.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (regression, -29.73%): Splitting the RVFI 64-bit counter into low/high halves did not guarantee a shorter synthesized feedback path, and the low-half terminal compare plus high-half enable can add enough FPGA control/routing cost to overwhelm the intended carry-chain reduction.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -57.55%): Confidence gating did not make a two-entry loop-target predictor cheap enough because queue-head `if_head_*` lookup, `predict_fire` target selection, and carried prediction metadata still widen the fetch/redirect control path; predictor selectivity cannot offset front-end timing overhead unless the prediction transaction is isolated from ordinary `imem_addr` selection.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 (broken, n/a): A radix-4 early-out divider cannot be bolted onto EX by merely substituting `ALU_ADD` plus `divider_start/busy/done`; the ID/EX hold, EX/MEM commit, and hazard/RVFI-valid handshake must make the completed quotient or remainder one atomic result or cosimulation fails.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): A registered memory request/response boundary must distinguish request acceptance from returned-data validity and carry that state through load-use forwarding and RVFI retirement; conflating them can pass stores while every load form and dependent register check fail.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r11s2 (broken, n/a): Retirement-only RAS updates must remain aligned with the valid IF queue head and its carried prediction marker across stalls and flushes, or wrong-path calls/returns can consume a different stack top and break CoreMark cosimulation.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r12s0 (regression, -71.78%): Reconstructing REM with a quotient-times-divisor multiply after a shared `/` added another wide arithmetic cone plus sign/magnitude logic, so algebraic divider sharing can worsen FPGA timing unless the quotient and remainder are produced by one genuinely shared or iterative datapath.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.30%): A table-free direct-JAL predictor still regressed when every accepted response added opcode/target arithmetic plus prediction metadata through the two-entry queue and ID/EX, showing that response-side speculation must earn back its queue-control and bundle-routing cost even when it leaves the steady-state imem address path untouched.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r13s0 (regression, -23.18%): Splitting RV32I and RV32M into separate `always_comb` result cones did not isolate the CoreMark fast path because the wide multiply/divide hardware remains active and the explicit M-range result mux adds routing, so datapath partitioning must be validated against synthesized cones and placement rather than module boundaries.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r13s1 (regression, -35.34%): A fall-through fetch response from `imem_data`/`imem_ready` into IF/ID, plus effective-availability hazard logic, can widen the combinational fetch/decode control path enough to outweigh eliminating empty-queue refill bubbles, so bypasses must be validated against post-synthesis timing and benchmark IPC.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r13s2 (broken, n/a): A registered RAS response cannot be treated as a decode-only `prediction_wait`; queue pop, prediction-token capture, fetch-target update, and EX recovery must share one valid transaction or a stalled return loses instruction identity and fails RVFI `pc_fwd` and register checks.
- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r14s0 (regression, -18.50%): Qualifying both EX/MEM forwarding selects with `!mem_to_reg` regressed CoreMark, showing that added producer-control gating and fanout can outweigh pruning an unusable load-address bypass, so narrow forwarding optimizations still require post-synthesis timing validation.

